  "method": "resolved offline through the real EDGAR path \u2014 registrant SIC from the live submissions index, then the subject's own filing text \u2014 exactly as a run resolves it. Zero demo analyses spent. These are PREDICTIONS to score the rendered pages against, not readings of them.",
  "finding": "The class gate is not the bar \u00a744 and \u00a745 ask about. Sixteen companies resolve to nine classes, and one class holds six of them: Apple, AMD, Intel, Broadcom, Micron and NVIDIA are all DESIGN_AND_MANUFACTURE with a BYTE-IDENTICAL 10-pattern menu. Alphabet and Meta share ADVERTISING_PLATFORM; Microsoft and Salesforce share SUBSCRIPTION_SOFTWARE.",
  "what_this_does_and_does_not_show": "It does NOT show that these companies will read alike. Window 1 measured three companies with DIFFERENT menus differing 9/10, which says the class tables carry differentiation downstream of the menu. Nobody has yet measured two SAME-CLASS companies against each other, and that \u2014 not the menu \u2014 is the \u00a745 bar. The cheapest decisive pair is Microsoft vs Salesforce or NVIDIA vs AMD.",
  "what_would_be_the_wrong_fix": "Adding a bespoke class per company. \u00a744 forbids it without evidence, and it would reproduce the original defect in a new shape: twelve classes were already too coarse for Meta and too fine for nobody. If same-class companies do read alike, the differentiation has to come from each run's OWN evidence \u2014 its ladder, its XBRL metrics, its competitive ground \u2014 not from a seventeenth table row.",
